-saving-device-that-helps-people-escape-deadly-fires/articleshow/133643482.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/meet-kim-ji-on-a-12-year-old-south-korean-student-who-invented-a-life-saving-device-that-helps-people-escape-deadly-fires/articleshow/133643482.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-31T11:32:45+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133643697,imgsize-1245333.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Washington watershed sees chum salmon surge as river restoration nears completion]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133629632.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/in-the-early-1990s-fewer-than-1000-threatened-chum-salmon-returned-to-this-washington-watershed-recent-returns-average-32600-a-year-as-a-9-7-million-river-restoration-nears-completion/articleshow/133629632.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/in-the-early-1990s-fewer-than-1000-threatened-chum-salmon-returned-to-this-washington-watershed-recent-returns-average-32600-a-year-as-a-9-7-million-river-restoration-nears-completion/articleshow/133629632.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-31T11:20:31+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI Science Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133629677,imgsize-118413.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Alaska eliminates invasive pike and restocks lake with 6,836 Arctic char]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133628680.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/alaska-used-rotenone-to-eliminate-invasive-pike-from-an-entire-lake-the-pike-vanished-and-6836-arctic-char-were-later-stocked-to-rebuild-the-native-fishery/articleshow/133628680.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/alaska-used-rotenone-to-eliminate-invasive-pike-from-an-entire-lake-the-pike-vanished-and-6836-arctic-char-were-later-stocked-to-rebuild-the-native-fishery/articleshow/133628680.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-31T11:20:20+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133628781,imgsize-155572.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Osaka residents ate dozens of species in the 1600s, evidence stayed in teeth]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133642740.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[Teeth unearthed in Osaka have unveiled a diverse dietary landscape of commoners, revealing that their dental calculus held traces of proteins from fish, mammals, and plants. This discovery suggests that they enjoyed access to a variety of foods, including terrestrial meats, thanks to Osaka's strategic position as a distribution center. This research provides a rare window into the food culture and accessibility of premodern Japan.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/in-the-1600s-people-in-osaka-ate-dozens-of-species-of-fish-mammals-and-plants-400-years-later-scientists-found-the-evidence-trapped-in-their-teeth/articleshow/133642740.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/in-the-1600s-people-in-osaka-ate-dozens-of-species-of-fish-mammals-and-plants-400-years-later-scientists-found-the-evidence-trapped-in-their-teeth/articleshow/133642740.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-31T12:50:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133642865,imgsize-156920.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Meet the Texas students transforming toxic Martian soil for future astronauts]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133609355.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/meet-varshini-gandreddy-dhiti-koyya-and-raaga-bukkaraju-texas-students-turning-toxic-martian-soil-into-fertile-ground-for-astronauts/articleshow/133609355.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/meet-varshini-gandreddy-dhiti-koyya-and-raaga-bukkaraju-texas-students-turning-toxic-martian-soil-into-fertile-ground-for-astronauts/articleshow/133609355.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-31T10:00:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI Science Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133609601,imgsize-235906.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Meet the students who built DiveGuard, a low-cost underwater messaging system]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133630176.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[A group of Chinese students has created DiveGuard, an affordable system designed for underwater communication. By utilizing acoustic signals, this innovative technology allows divers to send concise text messages, thereby enhancing safety and teamwork in aquatic environments. DiveGuard was recognized as a finalist in the prestigious 2026 Conrad Challenge and offers a reliable communication alternative where traditional networks are unavailable.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/meet-hao-ting-lyu-qing-ying-cao-bai-xuan-ye-yuanhao-zhang-and-jia-an-pan-chinese-students-who-built-a-low-cost-underwater-messaging-system-for-divers/articleshow/133630176.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/meet-hao-ting-lyu-qing-ying-cao-bai-xuan-ye-yuanhao-zhang-and-jia-an-pan-chinese-students-who-built-a-low-cost-underwater-messaging-system-for-divers/articleshow/133630176.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-31T07:00:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI Science Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133630311,imgsize-1136885.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[How 3,000 giant tortoises are rebuilding Española after near extinction]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133630102.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/in-the-1970s-just-14-giant-tortoises-remained-on-espaola-now-3000-are-restoring-vegetation-and-bringing-albatrosses-back-to-nesting-grounds/articleshow/133630102.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/in-the-1970s-just-14-giant-tortoises-remained-on-espaola-now-3000-are-restoring-vegetation-and-bringing-albatrosses-back-to-nesting-grounds/articleshow/133630102.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-31T00:00:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI Science Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133630244,imgsize-2505120.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Chimpanzees keep throwing rocks at same trees for a decade, scientists probe why]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133641943.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[In an intriguing study, scientists are examining the unusual habit of chimpanzees who consistently throw stones at designated trees. This behaviour, predominantly seen in adult males accompanied by their loud vocalisations, might indicate a method of communication or territorial assertion. The sustained use of these locations hints at a learned tradition being handed down over generations, shedding light on both primate culture and possible connections to human evolution.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/chimpanzees-have-been-throwing-rocks-at-the-same-trees-for-more-than-a-decade-scientists-are-trying-to-solve-the-mystery/articleshow/133641943.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/chimpanzees-have-been-throwing-rocks-at-the-same-trees-for-more-than-a-decade-scientists-are-trying-to-solve-the-mystery/articleshow/133641943.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-31T12:00:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI Science Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133642207,imgsize-483872.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Centuries-old plaque on eight people’s teeth preserved a surprisingly detailed menu of what they ate in Osaka]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133641273.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[Dental plaque analysis revealed diverse diets for Edo-period Osaka commoners. Researchers found proteins from 17 food types, including luxury red sea bream. This suggests developing markets made once-exclusive foods accessible to ordinary people. Salmon proteins indicated food traveled long distances via extensive shipping networks. Even cottonseed oil and terrestrial animal meat were consumed by these individuals.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/researchers-analysing-hardened-plaque-from-eight-people-buried-in-edo-era-osaka-detected-38-dietary-proteins-including-luxury-red-sea-bream-and-cold-water-fish-imported-through-japans-shipping-networks/articleshow/133641273.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/researchers-analysing-hardened-plaque-from-eight-people-buried-in-edo-era-osaka-detected-38-dietary-proteins-including-luxury-red-sea-bream-and-cold-water-fish-imported-through-japans-shipping-networks/articleshow/133641273.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-31T09:44:31+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI Lifestyle Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133641300,imgsize-1743594.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[James Cagney bought 417 acres of California land that became a wildlife corridor]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133641533.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/in-1940-actor-james-cagney-bought-hundreds-of-acres-of-california-grassland-decades-later-the-remaining-417-acres-became-a-protected-wildlife-corridor/articleshow/133641533.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/in-1940-actor-james-cagney-bought-hundreds-of-acres-of-california-grassland-decades-later-the-remaining-417-acres-became-a-protected-wildlife-corridor/articleshow/133641533.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-31T10:30:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133642162,imgsize-1693790.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[US mayor Joe Anderson dies mysteriously at 45 after sudden collapse on Colorado hike]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133641571.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[]]></descri